  "worked_solution": "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{\u23df}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{69} & =P{D}^{69}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{69}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{69} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{69}\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
